`,e.exports.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Lastly, a ",e.exports.jsx(s.strong,{children:"filter"})," changes a signal by preserving some frequencies and suppressing others. A ",e.exports.jsx(s.strong,{children:"low-pass filter"})," keeps low frequencies and removes high frequencies, producing a smoother or blurrier signal. On the other hand, a ",e.exports.jsx(s.strong,{children:"high-pass filter"})," emphasizes rapid changes. Before sampling, we often apply a ",e.exports.jsx(s.strong,{children:"low-pass filter"})," to remove details with high frequencies that the sampling process cannot represent after reconstruction. This is called ",e.exports.jsx(s.strong,{children:"pre-filtering"}),"."]}),`
`,e.exports.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Connecting back to the concept of multiple domains previously, operations in one domain have related effects in the other. Specifically, ",e.exports.jsx(s.strong,{children:"multiplication in the frequency domain"})," corresponds to ",e.exports.jsx(s.strong,{children:"convolution in the spatial domain"}),". Thus, multiplying a frequency spectrum by a low-pass filter is equivalent to convolving the original signal with a spatial-domain blur kernel. This relationship is known as the ",e.exports.jsx(s.strong,{children:"Fourier duals"}),": a function in one domain has a corresponding function in the other. Please refer to the ",e.exports.jsx(s.a,{href:"/lectures",children:"lecture slides"})," if you need a refresher on this topic."]}),`
